@code_type rust .rs
@comment_type // %s
@title Mini-index pagination fixture

@s Definitions

The first section gives the later pages several meanings to refer back to.

--- fixture.rs
struct Alpha;
struct Beta;
struct Delta;
struct Epsilon;
struct Eta;
struct Gamma;
struct Iota;
struct Kappa;
struct Theta;
struct ZBoundary;

fn combine(value: Alpha) -> Alpha {
    value
}
---

@s One long presentation section

This deliberately long section continues across physical page boundaries.
The examples repeatedly use @code{Alpha}, @code{Beta}, and @code{Gamma} so a
spread keeps only one entry for each meaning.

The first explanation follows @code{Alpha} through a successful operation.
The companion values @code{Beta} and @code{Gamma} remain available while the
operation proceeds.

Another paragraph uses @code{Delta}, @code{Epsilon}, and @code{Eta}. These
names make the eventual three-column ordering easy to inspect.

The same calculation can be described again with @code{Alpha}. Repetition on
one spread must not duplicate its mini-index entry.

The next step reads @code{Iota}, @code{Kappa}, and @code{Theta}. Each name was
defined in the earlier code block rather than on this part of the document.

Ordinary prose fills the page without introducing another mechanism. Its
purpose is to let the presentation section cross a real page boundary.

The explanation returns to @code{Alpha}, @code{Beta}, and @code{Gamma}. A
reader on a later spread should be directed back to their definitions.

More prose keeps the running example together. The values move through the
same stages, but the document does not create new definitions for them.

The later stage uses @code{Delta}, @code{Epsilon}, and @code{Eta} once more.
Their entries should remain alphabetical rather than following this sentence.

A physical page may end in the middle of this presentation section. Litweb
must use page markers rather than assigning the whole section to every spread.

The following paragraph again mentions @code{Iota}, @code{Kappa}, and
@code{Theta}. It is intentionally separated from their declarations.

Several sentences now provide ordinary reading material. They make room for
the page composer to shorten a right page when its mini-index needs space.

The calculation still depends on @code{Alpha}. Its use remains a use even
though the name occurs in explanatory prose instead of a displayed block.

The next paragraph names @code{Beta} and @code{Gamma}, then continues with a
plain-language description of the same successful computation.

Another stage reads @code{Delta} and @code{Epsilon}. The mini-index should
link both entries to presentation section 1.

The remaining value @code{Eta} joins @code{Iota}. These uses are far enough
from the declarations to require help on a later spread.

The example continues without a new heading because it is still explaining
one operation. A presentation section is not assumed to fit on one page.

Now @code{Kappa} and @code{Theta} return. Their source spellings contain no
LaTeX commands, and the renderer assigns inert numeric marker identities.

One more paragraph uses @code{Alpha}, @code{Beta}, and @code{Gamma}. The
right-hand page must leave enough room below its body for the measured index.

The following ordinary paragraph helps exercise a page break near an
identifier marker. No authored text may be lost or duplicated by that break.

The next operation uses @code{Delta}, @code{Epsilon}, and @code{Eta}. If this
paragraph moves, its identifiers must move to the same later spread.

The final explanatory group uses @code{Iota}, @code{Kappa}, and @code{Theta}.
The page-local result should still be alphabetized in three columns.

The prose finishes by calling @code{combine()} with an @code{Alpha}. The
function and type are both defined in the earlier presentation section.

--- Later code
fn later(value: Alpha) -> Alpha {
    combine(value)
}
---

@s Material around a shortening boundary

The second half of the fixture continues the same example. It uses
@code{Alpha}, @code{Beta}, and @code{Gamma} after their definitions have moved
onto an earlier spread.

A reader can also meet @code{Delta}, @code{Epsilon}, and @code{Eta} here. The
three names should occupy different alphabetical positions without changing
the prose that surrounds them.

The operation next combines @code{Iota}, @code{Kappa}, and @code{Theta}.
Enough ordinary language follows to put a real page boundary near one of
these marked uses.

This paragraph describes a successful step without introducing an identifier.
It gives the page builder material that may stay on this spread or move to the
next one when the measured mini-index takes its space.

The explanation calls @code{later()} and then returns to @code{Alpha}. Because
@code{later()} was defined on this spread, its entry should be suppressed here
even though the older type may still need an entry.

Another ordinary paragraph keeps the example readable while exercising the
same page-composition path. The authored sentences must remain in their
original order after the right page is shortened.

The next successful step reads @code{Beta}, @code{Gamma}, and @code{Delta}.
Each name has already appeared, but one spread still receives at most one
entry for each meaning.

More explanation fills the physical page. Mini-index measurement may move
this paragraph, but it must never duplicate it or leave it behind under the
horizontal rule.

Four more paragraphs put a unique use close to the space claimed by the
mini-index. The first recalls why the body and the index must be measured as
one page instead of laid on top of each other.

The next paragraph contains no marked identifier. It can cross a page
boundary freely, but its words must retain the same order before and after the
composer returns displaced material to TeX.

A third paragraph supplies another few lines of ordinary prose. A stable
second LuaLaTeX run should choose exactly the same breaks as the first run
after links and destinations have settled.

The fourth paragraph brings the calculation close to the boundary without
naming a program item. The following sentence is the one whose membership is
easy to check in the finished PDF.

One unmarked paragraph adds enough material for the measured index to make a
real difference. Without the reserved rows it could remain on the right page;
with them, TeX must carry later sentences forward in source order.

A second paragraph continues that thought in ordinary language. It also makes
the fixture insensitive to tiny font-metric variations while keeping the
boundary check close enough to inspect by eye.

The last unmarked paragraph states the invariant plainly: shortening may
change a physical page membership, but it cannot change the authored program
or explanation that the reader receives.

Near the expected shortening boundary, the example mentions its boundary
value without naming the Rust type yet. Its marked use comes at the very end,
where the measured rows should carry it to the following spread.

The final paragraphs continue with @code{Eta}, @code{Iota}, and @code{Kappa}.
Their definitions are all in presentation section 1, so every printed entry
can link to a precise and useful destination.

One last operation mentions @code{Theta} and @code{combine()}. This ending is
long enough to leave material after the first right-hand page has reserved
space for its three balanced mini-index columns. The displaced final value has
type @code{ZBoundary}.
